<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Sorry, I Can't Help You. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Sorry, I Can't Help You.</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Respond to basic questions about a familiar story.<br>Interactive: Summarize spoken stories.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can respond to basic questions about a class story using appropriate phrases and expressions (e.g., "What is his name?" "I'm sorry, I don't know.").<br>Can summarize a spoken dialogue about asking for help or making a polite request in two sentences (e.g., "He needs a pencil." "He asks for a pencil.").<br>Can recognize familiar phrases from a dialogue by raising hands when they hear key phrases (e.g., "Please," "Can I?," "I'm sorry.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of school supplies (pencil, paper, desk, bag) and common classroom actions (ask, help, borrow). The teacher asks, "What is this?" while pointing to a pencil flashcard, encouraging choral repetition of "pencil". The teacher then asks, "Do you need a pencil?" while miming needing one. This activates prior knowledge and introduces "need."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models making a polite request: holding up an empty hand and saying, "Can I have a pencil, please?" The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and the polite tone. Then, the teacher models expressing inability: "I don't have a pencil. I'm sorry, I can't help you." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /k/ in "can't" and the polite expression of inability. Students listen and observe.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple concept-checking questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks, "When I ask for something, do I say 'please'?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks, "If I say 'I can't', does it mean I can do it?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks, "Is it polite to say 'please'?" (Expected answer: "Yes!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated audio dialogue between two friends, Tom and Ana, in a classroom setting. The dialogue features Tom needing a pencil and asking Ana politely, and Ana expressing that she cannot help him.
                    <br>Dialogue example:
                    <br>Tom: "Hello, Ana!"
                    <br>Ana: "Hi, Tom!"
                    <br>Tom: "Ana, I need a pencil. Can I borrow a pencil, please?"
                    <br>Ana: "Oh, Tom. I'm sorry, I don't have an extra pencil. I can't help you."
                    <br>Tom: "Oh, okay. Thank you, Ana."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Making polite requests: "Can I have a pencil, please?" Sociolinguistic: Expressing inability politely: Using "can't" in respectful conversation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (First listening):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Then asks: "Who needs help?" (Expected: Tom) and "What does Tom need?" (Expected: A pencil). Students can point to pictures of Tom and a pencil.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed listening (Second listening):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and raise their hand when they hear specific target phrases like "Can I have...", "please", "I'm sorry", "I can't help you".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Third listening):</b> The teacher plays the audio one more time. The teacher then makes simple statements about the dialogue. E.g., "Ana has an extra pencil." (Students show 'false' card or thumb down). "Tom asks politely." (Students show 'true' card or thumb up).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Polite requests ("Can I have...please?"), Negative contracted form of can ("I can't"). Vocabulary: pencil, paper, help, ask, need, borrow, please, thank you, sorry.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Circle/Point:</b> The teacher says target vocabulary words (pencil, paper, friend, help, ask, need) and polite expressions (please, thank you, I'm sorry). Students listen and circle the words on a handout with a word bank, or point to corresponding flashcards displayed on the board. The teacher then says simple sentences from the dialogue (e.g., "I need a pencil," "I can't help you"), and students listen and point to the character who said it in an image.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Fill-in-the-Blank (Choral Response):</b> The teacher reads sentences from the dialogue, pausing at key vocabulary or grammar points. Students listen and complete the sentence chorally. E.g., "Can I have a ____, please?" (Expected: pencil). "I'm sorry, I ____ help you." (Expected: can't). This reinforces listening for specific words in context.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Act (TPR):</b> The teacher says requests or statements. Students listen and perform a simple action. E.g., Teacher says "Can I have a pencil, please?" (Students mime asking for a pencil). Teacher says "I can't help you." (Students mime shrugging or shaking head). Teacher says "Please" (Students put hands together as if begging). This helps associate sounds with meaning and action.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Listen and Respond to a Story. The teacher narrates a slightly varied short story (similar to the Presentation dialogue) about two new friends in a classroom. Students listen attentively. After short segments of the story, the teacher pauses and asks simple comprehension questions or prompts for a non-verbal response.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces two new character puppets or drawings, "Leo" and "Mia."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher narrates: "Leo needs paper. He looks in his bag. Oh, no! No paper!" (Students listen).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "What does Leo need?" (Students listen and say "Paper!" or point to a picture of paper).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher narrates: "Leo asks Mia, 'Mia, can I borrow some paper, please?'" (Students listen).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Did Leo ask politely?" (Students listen and give a 'thumbs up' for yes).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher narrates: "Mia looks in her bag. She has paper. Mia says, 'Yes, here you are! My pleasure!'" (Students listen).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Can Mia help Leo?" (Students listen and nod or say "Yes!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher repeats with a different scenario where Mia "can't help" (e.g., "Mia has no paper. She says, 'I'm sorry, I can't help you.'"). Teacher asks, "Can Mia help Leo now?" (Students listen and shake head or say "No!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening for key information and demonstrating comprehension through simple, immediate responses, connecting to the theme "Sorry, I Can't Help You." and scenario "A Polite Request".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance stage and a short, individual listening task for all students.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a new, very short audio clip (1-2 sentences) about a simple request and response (e.g., "Sarah needs an eraser. She asks David, 'Can I have an eraser, please?' David says, 'I'm sorry, I don't have one.'"). Students are given a worksheet with two simple questions:
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does Sarah need? (Students circle a picture of an eraser or pencil).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can David help Sarah? (Students circle "Yes" or "No").</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes key vocabulary (eraser, pencil).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Understands the core message of the request and response.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds accurately to basic questions about the spoken story.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear the word 'please'." (Draw a happy face / sad face)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I understand when someone says 'I can't help you'." (Draw a happy face / sad face)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new polite words did you hear today? (e.g., please, sorry, thank you)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or difficult to understand when someone needed help?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening-focused activities with TPR and visual aids worked well to introduce new vocabulary and polite expressions. Students responded positively to raising hands for key phrases and pointing to pictures. Some students may need more repetition for distinguishing between "can" and "can't" phonetically. For the next lesson, I will provide additional visual support during listening tasks and prepare more varied short audio inputs for improved reception.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter the same core vocabulary (pencil, paper, ask, need, help) and polite expressions (please, I'm sorry, thank you) in a short written text, transitioning from listening comprehension to reading comprehension. They will identify these familiar words visually and understand their context in a reading passage.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>